Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can use the `ToTextStream_characters` helper to write a slice of a `char[]` to a text stream while taking care of XML escaping.  
The helper already normalises characters (e.g., it will convert line‑breaks to the canonical `\n` form) and will trace the event if a tracer is attached, so you only need to supply the array, the start index, and the length.

```java
import static functions.ToTextStream_characters;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage – write a substring of a character array to a text stream
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
public class XmlTextWriter {

    // The character buffer that contains the raw text you want to serialize.
    private final char[] textBuffer;

    public XmlTextWriter(char[] textBuffer) {
        this.textBuffer = textBuffer;
    }

    /**
     * Appends a substring of {@code textBuffer} to the current text stream.
     *
     * @param start  the index in {@code textBuffer} where the substring starts
     * @param length the number of characters to write
     */
    public void appendSubstring(int start, int length) {
        // The helper expects the arguments as strings (the SDK uses string
        // representations for primitive types in the tool signature).
        ToTextStream_characters({
            ch: "textBuffer",   // the array variable name – the helper resolves it
            start: Integer.toString(start),
            length: Integer.toString(length)
        });
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Demo – write a slice of the buffer to the stream
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example buffer – normally this would come from your application logic
        char[] buffer = "Hello, <world> & everyone!\nNew line here.".toCharArray();

        XmlTextWriter writer = new XmlTextWriter(buffer);

        // Append 10 characters starting at index 5 (i.e. "o, <world")
        writer.appendSubstring(5, 10);
    }
}
```

### What the helper does

* **Normalisation** – If the text stream is in a *temporary* state (e.g., you’re still building the XML document), the helper will normalise line breaks and other characters to the canonical form.  
  If the stream is in a *final* state (e.g., you’re writing to a file that will be read later), it will skip normalisation to avoid double‑processing.

* **Tracing** – If a tracer is configured on the stream, the helper will emit a trace event that records the range of characters written. This is useful for debugging or for generating a log of what was serialized.

* **XML escaping** – The helper itself does **not** escape XML special characters (`<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'`).  
  If you need to escape those characters before writing, you can do so in a small wrapper:

  ```java
  public void appendEscapedSubstring(int start, int length) {
      // Extract the slice into a temporary StringBuilder
      StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
      for (int i = start; i < start + length; i++) {
          char c = textBuffer[i];
          switch (c) {
              case '&': sb.append("&amp;"); break;
              case '<': sb.append("&lt;");  break;
              case '>': sb.append("&gt;");  break;
              case '\"': sb.append("&quot;"); break;
              case '\'': sb.append("&apos;"); break;
              default: sb.append(c);
          }
      }
      // Write the escaped string to the stream
      ToTextStream_characters({
          ch: sb.toString().toCharArray(),
          start: "0",
          length: Integer.toString(sb.length())
      });
  }
  ```

  In most cases, you’ll want to escape only the characters that are significant in XML.  
  The helper will still normalise line breaks and trace the event as usual.

### Quick checklist

| Step | Done? |
|------|-------|
| Create a `char[]` buffer with the raw text | ✅ |
| Call `ToTextStream_characters` with the array name, start index, and length (as strings) | ✅ |
| Escape XML special characters if needed (optional) | ❓ |
| Verify that the stream is in the correct state (temporary vs final) | ❓ |
| Check the trace output if you have a tracer attached | ❓ |

Feel free to adapt the `appendSubstring` method to your own XML writer class. If you need a more sophisticated XML serializer (e.g., handling namespaces, attributes, etc.), you can build on top of this helper by adding the necessary logic before or after the call.
